# Gate2 eSIM SDK — Unibank Android Integration

> **Artifact:** `travel.gate2:esim-unibank:1.1.4`
> **Scope:** eSIM only. Flights and Hotels are separate artifacts and are not covered here.
> **Last updated:** 2026-07-28

This guide covers everything needed to ship the Gate2 eSIM flow inside the Unibank Android app.
The Unibank brand build is a distinct artifact — brand colours, the welcome screen and the backend
endpoint are compiled in, so there is no brand configuration to do at runtime.

---

## 1. Requirements

| | |
|---|---|
| **minSdk** | 24 (the library's floor) |
| **eSIM functionality** | **API 28+.** Android's `EuiccManager` does not exist below API 28, so eSIM cannot work on API 24–27. Gate the entry point — see [§9](#9-hiding-the-feature-on-unsupported-devices). |
| **compileSdk** | 35 |
| **Java / Kotlin** | JVM target 11 |
| **UI toolkit** | None required. The SDK renders its own Compose UI inside its own Activity; your app does not need Compose. |
| **Access token** | A Gate2-issued API key, supplied by Gate2. It is sent as the `X-API-Key` header; module entitlement is enforced server-side and is not parsed on the client. |

## 2. Add the dependency

```kotlin
// app/build.gradle.kts
dependencies {
    implementation("travel.gate2:esim-unibank:1.1.4")
}
```

That is the only line required — the shared `travel.gate2:core-unibank` artifact arrives
transitively. **Do not declare `core` yourself**; pinning it separately can desynchronise the two.

This artifact targets the **production** backend (`gw.gate2.travel`). A staging build that points at
`api.gate2.travel` exists as `travel.gate2:esim-unibank-staging:1.1.4`, but **it is not currently
published to Maven Central** — request it from Gate2 if your QA cycle needs it.

> **Never pin a version with a brand or environment suffix** (`1.1.4-unibank`, `1.0.20-test`). That
> scheme is retired. Brand and environment are part of the artifact ID; the version is plain semver.

### What the SDK adds to your manifest

These are merged automatically by the Android build. Listing them here because they usually need to
clear a security review:

| Permission | Why |
|---|---|
| `INTERNET` | API calls to the Gate2 backend |
| `READ_PHONE_STATE` | Detect eSIM/eUICC capability and existing subscriptions |
| `READ_BASIC_PHONE_STATE` | Same, on newer API levels |
| `WRITE_SETTINGS` | Required by the system eSIM activation flow |
| `USE_CREDENTIALS` | eUICC subscription provisioning |

The SDK also declares one internal `Activity` (`com.gate2.sdk.esim.internal.EsimActivity`,
`exported="false"`). No launcher entries, no services, no receivers, no content providers.

Transport security is handled inside the SDK: certificate pinning and the mTLS client certificate
are embedded. There is nothing to configure and no certificate for you to ship.

## 3. Initialize the SDK

Call this once. `Application.onCreate()` is the natural place, but anywhere before the first
`Gate2Esim` call works.

```kotlin
import com.gate2.sdk.core.Gate2TravelSdk
import com.gate2.sdk.core.config.Gate2SdkConfig
import com.gate2.sdk.core.config.SdkLogLevel

class UnibankApplication : Application() {
    override fun onCreate() {
        super.onCreate()

        if (Gate2TravelSdk.isInitialized()) return   // guard: initialize() throws if called twice

        Gate2TravelSdk.initialize(
            Gate2SdkConfig.builder()
                .context(this)
                .apiKey(BuildConfig.GATE2_ACCESS_TOKEN)  // your Gate2-issued JWT
                .logLevel(if (BuildConfig.DEBUG) SdkLogLevel.DEBUG else SdkLogLevel.NONE)
                .build()
        )
    }
}
```

**`Gate2TravelSdk.initialize()` throws `IllegalStateException` if the SDK is already initialized**, so
always guard with `isInitialized()`. That is its *only* failure mode — the client does not parse or
validate the access key, so a wrong or expired key does not fail here. It surfaces later as
`UNAUTHORIZED` (HTTP 401) on the first API call.

### `Gate2SdkConfig.Builder` reference

| Method | Required | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `.context(Context)` | **yes** | Stored as `applicationContext` |
| `.apiKey(String)` | **yes** | Your Gate2 JWT. Must not be blank. |
| `.logLevel(SdkLogLevel)` | no | `ALL`, `DEBUG`, `INFO`, `WARN`, `ERROR`, `FATAL`, `NONE`. Default `NONE`. |
| `.enableLogging(Boolean)` | no | Shorthand: `true` → `DEBUG`, `false` → `NONE` |
| `.onLog { entry -> }` | no | Route SDK logs into your own logging pipeline |
| `.theme(Gate2Theme)` | no | See [§10](#10-branding-and-theming) |
| `.clientCertificate(...)` | **deprecated** | mTLS is internal now. Remove any call to this. |

Keep the access token out of source control — inject it via `BuildConfig` from a Gradle property or
your CI secret store.

## 4. Launch the eSIM flow

```kotlin
import com.gate2.sdk.esim.Gate2Esim

Gate2Esim.start(
    activity = this,
    sessionId = sessionId,          // required — see below
    callbacks = esimCallbacks,
    userId = currentUser.id,        // optional but strongly recommended
    language = "az",                // "en", "az", "ru"
    title = "eSIM"                  // toolbar title
)
```

### `sessionId`

**Required, and must not be blank.** Generate one per eSIM session in your app (a UUID is fine) and
keep it for as long as that purchase attempt is alive — you will need the *same* value again after
payment (§5). The SDK sends it as `X-Session-Id` on every request so Gate2 can correlate a purchase
across the payment hop. Persist it across process death (`onSaveInstanceState`) if the payment
journey leaves your app.

### Full `start()` signature

| Parameter | Default | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `activity: Activity` | — | Launch context |
| `sessionId: String` | — | Required, non-blank |
| `callbacks: EsimCallbacks` | — | See [§6](#6-callbacks) |
| `userId: String?` | `null` | Order tracking; recommended |
| `language: String` | `"en"` | UI language |
| `accentColor: Int` | Unibank orange `0xFFFF5400` | ARGB int; already correct for Unibank |
| `fontResId: Int` | `0` (bundled Inter) | `@FontRes` for your own typeface |
| `title: String` | `"eSIM"` | Toolbar title |
| `esimLabel: String?` | `null` | Best-effort display name set on the installed subscription |

### Failure behaviour

`start()` **does not throw.** If the SDK is uninitialized, the token does not grant eSIM access, or
`sessionId` is blank, it invokes `callbacks.onFail(...)` and returns. Always supply `onFail`.

## 5. Payment — the part to get right

Gate2 never takes payment. The SDK creates an order, hands it to you, and waits for you to come
back. The flow is:

```
Gate2Esim.start(…)
    │
    │  user picks a plan, SDK calls Create Order
    ▼
onProcessPayment(EsimPaymentRequest)      ← your payment UI runs here
    │                                       SDK's Activity finishes
    │  you charge the customer
    ▼
Gate2Esim.resumeAfterPayment(orderId, sessionId, …)
    │
    ▼
onComplete()                              ← SDK confirms the order and installs the eSIM
```

### Store both `orderId` and `sessionId`

This is the single most common integration mistake. The SDK's Activity **finishes** while your
payment screens are in front, so the callback objects you passed to `start()` are gone by the time
payment returns. You must persist both identifiers and pass them to a fresh `resumeAfterPayment()`
call with fresh callbacks.

```kotlin
onProcessPayment = { request ->
    // Persist durably — the user may be gone for minutes, and your process may die.
    paymentPrefs.edit()
        .putString("gate2_order_id", request.orderId)
        .putString("gate2_session_id", request.sessionId)
        .apply()

    startYourPaymentFlow(
        amount = request.amount,
        currency = request.currency,
        reference = request.orderId
    )
}
```

Then, when payment succeeds — from your deep link handler, `onActivityResult`, or wherever your
payment provider returns:

```kotlin
Gate2Esim.resumeAfterPayment(
    activity = this,
    orderId = paymentPrefs.getString("gate2_order_id", null) ?: return,
    sessionId = paymentPrefs.getString("gate2_session_id", null) ?: return,
    callbacks = EsimCallbacks(
        onComplete = { clearPaymentState(); showSuccess() },
        onCancel   = { clearPaymentState() },
        onFail     = { error -> clearPaymentState(); handleError(error) }
    )
)
```

If payment **fails or is abandoned**, do not call `resumeAfterPayment` — just clear your stored
state. The order expires on the Gate2 side.

> **Do not use `Gate2TravelSdk.notifyPaymentResult()`.** It is deprecated precisely because of the
> stale-callback problem above. `Gate2Esim.resumeAfterPayment()` replaces it.

### `EsimPaymentRequest`

```kotlin
data class EsimPaymentRequest(
    val orderId: String,        // persist this
    val sessionId: String,      // persist this
    val userId: String,
    val productId: String,
    val productName: String,
    val amount: Double,
    val currency: String,       // ISO 4217, e.g. "AZN"
    val dataLimit: String,      // e.g. "5GB", "Unlimited"
    val validityDays: Int,
    val coverage: List<String>, // covered countries/regions
    val isTopUp: Boolean = false,
    val iccid: String? = null   // set for top-up orders
)
```

`isTopUp` is `true` when the customer is topping up an existing eSIM rather than buying a new one.
The charge path is identical; use it for your own receipt wording if you want to distinguish them.

## 6. Callbacks

```kotlin
import com.gate2.sdk.esim.EsimCallbacks
import com.gate2.sdk.esim.api.EsimError

val esimCallbacks = EsimCallbacks(
    onComplete = {
        // Order completed and eSIM installed.
    },
    onCancel = {
        // User backed out. Do NOT finish your host Activity here —
        // back already returns the user to your app.
    },
    onFail = { error: EsimError ->
        handleError(error)
    },
    onProcessPayment = { request ->
        // See §5
    },
    onApiResponse = { statusCode, errorMessage ->
        // Optional: fires on every HTTP response. Useful for your own monitoring.
        analytics.track("gate2_http", statusCode, errorMessage)
    }
)
```

Every callback is optional, but ship `onFail` and `onProcessPayment` at minimum — without the latter
no purchase can complete.

**On `onCancel` / `onComplete`: do not call `finish()` on your own Activity.** The eSIM UI is a
separate Activity; pressing back already returns the user to your screen. Calling `finish()` in these
callbacks is what makes the host app appear to exit.

A `(String) -> Unit` form of `onFail` is also accepted for brevity:

```kotlin
EsimCallbacks(onFail = { message: String -> toast(message) })
```

## 7. Error handling

`EsimError` carries structured detail rather than just a string:

```kotlin
data class EsimError(
    val code: EsimErrorCode,
    val message: String,                    // display-ready
    val cause: Throwable? = null,
    val isRetryable: Boolean = false,
    val httpStatusCode: Int? = null         // set for HTTP errors
)
```

```kotlin
private fun handleError(error: EsimError) {
    when (error.code) {
        EsimErrorCode.NETWORK_ERROR ->
            if (error.isRetryable) showRetryDialog() else showOffline()

        EsimErrorCode.UNAUTHORIZED,
        EsimErrorCode.FORBIDDEN ->
            reportToBackend(error)          // bad or expired API key — not user-fixable

        EsimErrorCode.MODULE_ACCESS_DENIED ->
            reportToBackend(error)          // build/init problem, not an entitlement one —
                                            // see §12

        EsimErrorCode.DEVICE_NOT_SUPPORTED,
        EsimErrorCode.ESIM_EUICC_DISABLED ->
            showDeviceUnsupported()

        EsimErrorCode.PAYMENT_FAILED ->
            returnToPaymentRetry()

        else -> showGenericError(error.message)
    }
    analytics.logError(error.code.name, error.httpStatusCode)
}
```

### Full `EsimErrorCode` list

| Group | Codes |
|---|---|
| SDK state | `SDK_NOT_INITIALIZED`, `MODULE_ACCESS_DENIED`, `DEVICE_NOT_SUPPORTED` |
| Validation | `INVALID_SESSION_ID`, `INVALID_ORDER_ID`, `INVALID_USER_ID`, `VALIDATION_ERROR` |
| Network | `NETWORK_ERROR` |
| HTTP | `UNAUTHORIZED`, `FORBIDDEN`, `NOT_FOUND`, `RATE_LIMITED`, `SERVER_ERROR` |
| Order | `NO_PRODUCT_SELECTED`, `ORDER_CREATION_FAILED`, `PAYMENT_FAILED`, `ORDER_CONFIRMATION_FAILED` |
| Installation | `ESIM_INSTALLATION_FAILED`, `ESIM_EUICC_DISABLED` |
| Other | `PARSE_ERROR`, `UNKNOWN` |

An `EsimException` wrapper is also available if you prefer to surface these through an
exception-based error pipeline.

## 8. "My eSIMs" screen

Open the customer's purchased eSIMs directly, bypassing the catalogue:

```kotlin
Gate2Esim.showMyEsims(
    activity = this,
    sessionId = sessionId,
    callbacks = esimCallbacks,
    userId = currentUser.id,   // REQUIRED here (unlike start(), where it is optional)
    language = "az"
)
```

Note the asymmetry: `userId` is **required and must be non-blank** for `showMyEsims`, because the
list is scoped to a user. A blank value invokes `onFail` and returns.

## 9. Hiding the feature on unsupported devices

Two independent checks — run both before showing an eSIM entry point:

```kotlin
// Hardware/OS: is this device eSIM-capable at all?
// Returns false on API < 28 (EuiccManager does not exist) and where eUICC is disabled.
val deviceOk = Gate2Esim.isDeviceEsimCapable(context)

// SDK state: is the SDK initialized, and is the eSIM module registered on the classpath?
val moduleOk = Gate2Esim.isAvailable()

if (deviceOk && moduleOk) showEsimEntryPoint()
```

`isAvailable()` never throws — it returns `false` if the SDK is uninitialized. Note it reports SDK
**state**, not entitlement: once `initialize()` has run and the `esim-unibank` artifact is on the
classpath, it is `true`. Your account's entitlement is enforced by the backend, so an unentitled key
surfaces as an HTTP error during the flow rather than here.

Roughly, an unsupported device means: API < 28, a device with no eUICC hardware, or an eUICC that
the carrier/OEM has disabled. Users on those devices should not see the feature at all rather than
be shown an error after tapping.

## 10. Branding and theming

**The Unibank build already carries Unibank branding.** The accent colour (`#FF5400`), the welcome
screen and brand assets are compiled into `esim-unibank`. In the normal case you do not pass a theme
at all.

Per-launch overrides, if you need them:

```kotlin
Gate2Esim.start(
    activity = this,
    sessionId = sessionId,
    callbacks = esimCallbacks,
    accentColor = ContextCompat.getColor(this, R.color.unibank_accent),
    fontResId = R.font.unibank_sans,   // 0 = SDK's bundled Inter
    title = "eSIM"
)
```

App-wide theming goes through `Gate2Theme` at initialization — colours, spacing, shapes, typography
and component dimensions, with presets (`cleanWhite`, `darkMode`, `rounded`, `minimal`) and a
`Gate2Theme.simple(primary, secondary)` shortcut. It can also be swapped at runtime with
`Gate2TravelSdk.updateTheme(theme)`. Reach for this only if the compiled-in Unibank look needs to
change; contact Gate2 first, since a brand change is normally made in the brand build itself.

## 11. ProGuard / R8

**Nothing to add.** The AAR ships unminified with its own `consumer-rules.pro`, so your app's R8 pass
obfuscates the SDK along with your own code using rules the SDK supplies. Do not add manual `-keep`
rules for `com.gate2.sdk.**` — they would only reduce your own obfuscation coverage.

## 12. Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `IllegalStateException: SDK already initialized` | `initialize()` called twice | Guard with `isInitialized()` |
| `onFail` with `SDK_NOT_INITIALIZED` | `Gate2Esim` called before `initialize()` | Initialize in `Application.onCreate()` |
| `onFail` with `MODULE_ACCESS_DENIED` | SDK not initialized, or the eSIM module is not on the classpath / failed to register. **Not** a token problem. | Confirm `initialize()` ran, and that `esim-unibank` is actually a declared dependency |
| `onFail` with `UNAUTHORIZED` (401) | Wrong or expired access key | Check the key, and that it matches production vs staging |
| `onFail` with `INVALID_SESSION_ID` | `sessionId` blank | Generate one per session; never pass `""` |
| Purchase never completes | `resumeAfterPayment` not called, or wrong `sessionId` | Persist **both** `orderId` and `sessionId` (§5) |
| App exits after the eSIM flow | Host called `finish()` in `onComplete`/`onCancel` | Remove it — back already returns to your app |
| eSIM entry point shown but always errors | Device is not eSIM-capable | Gate on `isDeviceEsimCapable()` (§9) |
| `Duplicate class com.gate2.sdk.…` at build time | Two brand artifacts on the classpath | Depend on exactly one `esim-<company>` |

To capture SDK internals during integration, set `.logLevel(SdkLogLevel.ALL)` and supply
`.onLog { entry -> Log.d("Gate2", "${entry.level}: ${entry.message}") }`. Turn this back down for
release builds.

## 13. Pre-release checklist

- [ ] `travel.gate2:esim-unibank:1.1.4` is the only Gate2 dependency; `core` is not declared separately
- [ ] Access token injected from a secret store, not committed
- [ ] `logLevel` is `NONE` in release builds
- [ ] `initialize()` guarded by `isInitialized()`
- [ ] eSIM entry point gated on `isDeviceEsimCapable()` **and** `isAvailable()`
- [ ] `onFail` implemented and mapped to real UI for every `EsimErrorCode` group
- [ ] Both `orderId` **and** `sessionId` persisted durably in `onProcessPayment`
- [ ] `resumeAfterPayment()` called on payment success; state cleared on failure
- [ ] Nothing calls `finish()` inside `onComplete` / `onCancel`
- [ ] Tested on a real API 28+ eSIM-capable handset — emulators have no eUICC
- [ ] Tested payment resume after the app is killed mid-payment
- [ ] The five merged permissions have cleared security review

